DPDFNET-MIC-BRIDGE
==================

dpdfnet-mic-bridge takes your real microphone, runs DPDFNet enhancement on it,
then sends the cleaned audio into a virtual microphone that apps like
Discord, OBS, or games can use.

    physical mic -> native Rust/PortAudio engine -> Python DPDFNet worker -> native Rust/PortAudio engine -> virtual mic -> Discord/OBS/game

The real-time audio engine is native Rust using PortAudio. Python is only the
control, device-selection, CLI, and DPDFNet model layer. There is no sounddevice
runtime dependency and no Python PortAudio callback.

DPDFNet:
    https://github.com/ceva-ip/DPDFNet


INSTALL UV
==========

Install uv first:

    https://docs.astral.sh/uv/getting-started/installation/


INSTALL NATIVE BUILD PREREQUISITES
==================================

uv sync builds the Rust extension with maturin, so you need a Rust toolchain and
PortAudio development files before installing this project.

Rust:

    https://rustup.rs/

Linux examples:

    sudo apt install pkg-config portaudio19-dev

or on Fedora-like systems:

    sudo dnf install pkgconf-pkg-config portaudio-devel

Windows notes:

    Install the Visual Studio C++ build tools and a Rust MSVC toolchain.
    Install PortAudio with vcpkg or provide a PortAudio library yourself.
    If needed, set:

        PORTAUDIO_LIB_DIR=<directory containing the PortAudio library>
        PORTAUDIO_LIB_NAME=<library name without platform prefix/suffix>

Then open a terminal in the dpdfnet-mic-bridge project folder and run:

    uv sync

This installs Python dependencies and compiles/installs the native Rust
PortAudio extension as:

    dpdfnet_mic_bridge._native_audio


SET UP YOUR VIRTUAL MICROPHONE
==============================


WINDOWS
-------

Install VB-CABLE first:

    https://vb-audio.com/Cable/

After installing it, you should have endpoints like:

    CABLE Input
    CABLE Output

The bridge writes audio into:

    CABLE Input

Apps should use this as the microphone:

    CABLE Output

So in Discord, OBS, games, and other apps, choose CABLE Output as the mic.


LINUX WITH PIPEWIRE
-------------------

Create the virtual mic target first:

    pactl load-module module-null-sink media.class=Audio/Source/Virtual sink_name=dpdfnet_mic sink_properties=device.description=DPDFNet_Mic channels=1 channel_map=mono

Apps should select this as the microphone:

    DPDFNet_Mic

Some apps may show the matching source or monitor instead. Pick the one
that receives the bridge audio.


START THE BRIDGE
================

From the project folder, run:

    uv run dpdfnet-mic-bridge

Leave that terminal open while using your mic.

Stop it with:

    Ctrl+C


CHOOSE THE VIRTUAL MIC IN YOUR APP
==================================

In Discord, OBS, a game, or another app:

On Windows, choose:

    CABLE Output

On Linux, choose:

    DPDFNet_Mic

Your app should now hear the enhanced mic audio instead of the raw
physical mic.


WHEN AUTO DEVICE SELECTION GETS CONFUSED
========================================

List devices:

    uv run dpdfnet-mic-bridge devices

This shows native PortAudio device names and IDs.

Then run with a specific input and output:

    uv run dpdfnet-mic-bridge --input <mic-id> --output <virtual-mic-id>

Example:

    uv run dpdfnet-mic-bridge --input 15 --output 19

Use your real microphone as --input.

Use the virtual target as --output, such as CABLE Input on Windows or
the DPDFNet_Mic target on Linux.

Device IDs can change after rebooting, unplugging devices, or audio
backend changes. When a pinned command stops working, rerun:

    uv run dpdfnet-mic-bridge devices


USUAL FULL FLOW
===============


WINDOWS
-------

    uv sync
    uv run dpdfnet-mic-bridge

Then select CABLE Output in Discord, OBS, a game, or another app.


LINUX PIPEWIRE
--------------

    uv sync
    pactl load-module module-null-sink media.class=Audio/Source/Virtual sink_name=dpdfnet_mic sink_properties=device.description=DPDFNet_Mic channels=1 channel_map=mono
    uv run dpdfnet-mic-bridge

Then select DPDFNet_Mic in Discord, OBS, a game, or another app.
